meta {
  name: List User Options (for pickers)
  type: http
  seq: 11
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/options
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("is a bare array, not a paginated envelope", function () {
    // Deliberate: a picker renders every option at once. `GET /users` is the
    // one that paginates.
    expect(res.getBody()).to.be.an("array");
  });

  test("carries only what a <Select> renders", function () {
    const row = res.getBody()[0];
    expect(row).to.have.property("_id");
    expect(row).to.have.property("email");
    // No roles, no branch, no status, no invite token.
    expect(row).to.not.have.property("roleIds");
    expect(row).to.not.have.property("deactivatedAt");
    expect(row).to.not.have.property("inviteToken");
    expect(row).to.not.have.property("passwordHash");
  });

  test("excludes the platform admin", function () {
    // `adminEmail` is the seeded super admin. Both this filter and the
    // active-only one used to run in the browser, in three separate components.
    const all = res.getBody().map(function (row) { return row.email; });
    expect(all).to.not.include(bru.getEnvVar("adminEmail"));
  });

  test("the owner, who is active, is in it", function () {
    const all = res.getBody().map(function (row) { return row.email; });
    expect(all).to.include(bru.getEnvVar("ownerEmail"));
  });

  test("is sorted the way a person scans a list", function () {
    const names = res.getBody().map(function (row) {
      return ((row.lastName || "") + " " + (row.firstName || "") + " " + row.email)
        .trim()
        .toLowerCase();
    });
    const sorted = names.slice().sort();
    expect(names).to.deep.equal(sorted);
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /users/options — the assignable-people picker (PAC-101)

  - **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`)
  - **Permission:** `agency:users:read` — the same gate as the directory.
  - **Data scope:** the caller's agency.

  ## Why this exists separately from `GET /users`

  They answer different questions. `GET /users` is the **agency directory**:
  paginated, searchable, and showing everybody — pending invites and
  de-provisioned accounts included, because the whole point of that screen is
  acting on them. A picker wants the opposite: the **whole list at once**, and
  only people it makes sense to assign work to.

  A picker that paginates is worse UX than the unpaginated roster it replaced,
  and one showing page 1 of 3 is a bug. So rather than teach three `<Select>`
  components to page, PAC-101 split the two apart — which is also what let
  `GET /users` change shape without touching them.

  Three callers: the Leads producer filter, the Lead Owner reassignment picker,
  and the Deal Audit assignee picker. All three key it on `agency-users`.

  ## What it filters, server-side

  - **Platform admins** — not the agency's to assign.
  - **Anyone not `isActive`** — which excludes a **pending invite** as well as a
    removed employee. `isActive` is false for both and `deactivatedAt` is what
    separates them; nobody should be assigned work before accepting their
    invitation. This was the existing behaviour of all three pickers, done in
    the browser three times.

  Sorted `lastName, firstName, email` under the `en`/strength-2 collation, so
  "adams" does not sort after "Zimmer". `email` is the tiebreak because it is
  unique.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  [
    { "_id": "665f…ac1", "email": "producer@demo.local",
      "firstName": "Dana", "lastName": "Okafor" }
  ]
  ```

  `firstName` / `lastName` are both optional — an invited user who has not
  completed onboarding has neither, which is why the clients fall back to the
  email.

  ## Errors
  - `401` — missing or expired bearer token.
  - `403` — the caller lacks `agency:users:read`.
}
